16:The owner wants projects to run granularly, with their own chains and governance, behind a machine-level mesh service; projects inside a container-like boundary where WeftOS does lifecycle and coordination but not the project's development work; WeftOS runnable inside WeftOS; ESP32 and WASM supported; and a way for an organization to stand up a primary instance that distributes work to subordinates, with development, staging and production instances that are shaped differently.
29:| **Leaf / client** | one provisioned identity and one role | a chain; governance (enforced by its parent) |
31:Profiles: full shared host (service + a user daemon per user + a kernel per active project); personal host (machine and user roles in one `weaver`, project kernels as children); single-tenant host such as a Pi, container or server (one process, all roles); ESP32 leaf; browser client (behind the gateway, ADR-102 token, ephemeral certified key); WASI (leaf or collapsed host).
37:- **D3 User binding.** A user key is **bound** to the OS uid on first registration over a peer-credential-checked local socket (`getpeereid` / `SO_PEERCRED`). The binding is recorded in the machine journal; an admin can revoke and rebind.
38:- **D4 Runtime roots are machine-wide.** Machine role: `/var/lib/weftos` (state) and `/var/run/weftos` (sockets). Per-user state under `~/.weftos/` (`run/`, `projects/`, `chain/`). `~/.clawft/` becomes legacy, read for migration only. `WEFTOS_RUNTIME_DIR` remains the full-isolation override for tests, probes and nested instances.
42:- **D8 Governance overlays** are **tighten-only**: a project (and a nested instance) may add denies and lower limits, never relax the parent's rules. Every chain event records the effective-rule hash.
43:  - *D8 limits (Phase 2):* the kernel gate, the `agent.chat` tool gate and the spawned-agent gate follow live parent updates and `governance.reload`; the placement `WorkloadGate` is built from the effective rules when the control plane is built, so a push does not reach it until the kernel restarts. `max_processes` and `spawn_budget` (the concurrent sub-agent spawn cap) apply at boot; a push that changes them reports `restart_required`. The child trusts the user key in `<run>/<id>/user.pub` (written by the supervisor, outside the project tree) and falls back to its own certificate with a warning only while that pin is absent; this assumes nothing inside the project can write the run dir, which Phase 4 sandboxes must keep true.
44:- **D9 `project` is an ADR-099 workload kind** with pluggable sandbox drivers: `logical` (no sandbox, Phase 2), OpenShell or containers on Linux, Seatbelt or Apple `container` on macOS, wasmtime for WASM projects. The sandbox is the workload; the project kernel runs inside it as its supervisor; the user daemon supervises from outside (§6). *(Owner did not answer D9 explicitly; recorded as the analysis recommendation, reversible until Phase 4.)*
45:- **D10 Nested WeftOS** is isolated by default. A toggle, `weave.master = true`, marks an instance as the master of the WeftOS instances nested under it; the master manages their config (ports, registration level, governance cap). An inner instance never joins the outer mesh unless its master registers it.
49:- **D14 Resolution.** `weft` finds a kernel by flag → env → manifest → user default, then confirms with a `kernel.status` handshake returning `{node_id, user_id, project_id, depth, parent}`. It never maps CWD straight to a socket, never falls back silently to an in-process kernel, and prints what it tried.
79:  - *Phase 2:* one project key (node = chain = anchor key); invalid overlay at boot refuses to start; `idle_stop_secs` 1800 and never while agents, workloads or streams are active; children survive a user-daemon restart by adoption (`kernel stop` cascades unless `--keep-children`); mesh delivery to children is register-only until Phase 3; project chains start fresh with a parent-head link; existing projects opt in via `weaver project migrate-kernel`, default flips in Phase 3.
86:  - *Child root.* `RootSource::Child { id, project_root }`: ephemeral files under `~/.weftos/run/<id>/` (socket, pid, lock, log, `spawn.json`, `parent-policy.json`, `state.json`), durable files under `<root>/.weftos/` (`project.key`, `project.cert.json`, `chain/`, `state/`, committed `overlay.toml`). The project walk-up never selects a child.
87:  - *Overlay.* Tighten-only: `deny` and `require_approval` union, numeric limits `min(parent, overlay)`, booleans `parent OR overlay`; `permit`, `deactivate`, a parent rule id (compared trimmed and case-insensitively) or a looser limit is a hard error naming the key, and an invalid overlay refuses boot. The effective-rules hash is `SHA-256("weftos-effective-rules-v1\n" + canonical JSON of {parent_hash, overlay_hash, merged rules sorted by id, merged limits})` and rides on every child chain event as `rule_hash`.
91:  - *Child bootstrap, as built (package H).* `pre_boot` reads and deletes `spawn.json` (0600, 60 s, wall clock), loads `project.key`, registers over `mesh-local/1`, verifies and persists the certificate and verifies the signed parent policy before the chain is opened. The register acknowledgement is signed by the user key over child-chosen randomness, so a socket squatting at the parent path cannot forge one; registration is also bound by a second project-key signature over socket, pid and client nonce, and heartbeat and unregister carry a project-key signature over (op, session, pid, time), so a leaked session id is not a credential. The pid in `project.register` is recorded as `claimed_pid`: the child reports it and the user daemon does not verify it (it checks it against the pid the supervisor spawned, which the supervisor records on the spawn expectation right after starting the process; a registration that wins the race before that is not checked). Removal of `spawn.json` is by path after the descriptor checks. The anchor acknowledgement is not signed (transport-authenticated, as in A7 above). *Revocation reach:* `project.rekey` drops the session (the old key's re-register is refused `key_revoked`) and writes no marker, so the rekeyed child boots under its new certificate. `project.revoke` is terminal: it drops the session and writes `~/.weftos/run/<id>/revoked` (atomic, 0600; the run dir is created 0700 when the child is stopped), and nothing clears it, and since the Phase 2 review a revoked id never re-enrols at all (`project_revoked`, see *Phase 2 review* below). A running child whose re-register is refused `key_revoked`, `key_conflict`, `key_reuse`, `project_not_found` or `project_revoked` shuts the kernel down; a child booted while the parent is unreachable refuses only if the marker exists, so it cannot see a revocation issued while the user daemon could not write it. A challenge nonce that was evicted or used is refused `challenge_unknown` and the child asks again, for at most 45 s of retries (under the 60 s spawn window). A heartbeat proof covers the reported activity and is replayable for at most 30 s (liveness only). `--profile project` forces the project profile in `daemon::run`, so a project kernel cannot boot as a plain kernel.
93:  - *Project token (package G).* `TokenScope::Project`: `write` capability only, never Admin, bound to one project, TTL 3600 s, renewed through `project.token.refresh` (route `Write`) by the child's `ParentLink` when 900 s remain (before each call and from its monitor, at most one attempt per 30 s). The supervisor honours a refresh only for the token it issued last or the one before and revokes the older; stopping or failing a project revokes both. In addition `authorize_caller` refuses every method outside `project_token_scope::ALLOWED_METHODS` / `ALLOWED_PREFIXES` (`shared.`, `kernel.handshake`, `project.token.refresh`) with `project_token_method_denied`: what the `ParentLink` calls and nothing else. The child's other parent calls (`mesh.*`, `project.anchor.submit`) do not present the token. Honest limit: a same-uid caller sending the literal scope `admin` (ADR-070) bypasses this like every capability check until Phase 3 peer credentials. Known limitation: tokens live in the chain, which is saved only on a clean shutdown, so after an unclean user-daemon exit a surviving child's token is unknown, its refresh fails closed, and its shared services stay `down` until the child is restarted (`weaver kernel restart --project <id>`). Adoption cannot re-issue a token to a running child because there is no channel to deliver it.
94:  - *Adoption, revocation, markers (package G).* On boot the supervisor scans `<run>/*/kernel.pid` and adopts only a child whose pid is alive, whose executable name matches the one recorded in `state.json`, whose `kernel.lock` is held by that pid and whose `kernel.handshake` names the project and the same pid; an unverifiable leftover is listed (`weaver kernel status`, `weaver doctor`) and never signalled; a pid is re-verified before every signal (SIGTERM and SIGKILL), and an adopted pid is group-signalled only when it leads its group. A verified child of a project that no longer prepares is never left unmanaged: a revoked project's child is stopped, anything else is reported with the reason. `ensure_running` takes over a live verified child instead of starting a second. After a SIGHUP re-exec the daemon is still the parent of its children but has no waiter, so adopted pids are reaped with `waitpid(WNOHANG)` to tell a dead child from a zombie. G is the single writer of the terminal `<run>/<id>/revoked` marker: `project.revoke`'s identity hook (`on_identity_change`, after the journal write) writes it (0600, run dir 0700), and nothing ever clears it; the supervisor kills the child's credentials first, stops it (falling through to signals if the gated stop is refused), marks the project `failed` and never respawns it; `project.rekey` and a repair that changes a bound key stop the old child and write no marker.
96:  - *Parent transports (package G, H).* One transport for `project.anchor.submit`: H's `RpcParentTransport` (it verifies the parent socket's owner and mode and bounds the whole exchange). G's own was removed. `ParentLink` (shared services and token refresh) keeps its own bounded client because it is async and carries the project token.
97:  - *Integration (Phase 2).* The user daemon's `post_boot` installs the supervisor, runs the adoption scan and starts the idle loop. The only permit for the `project` kind stays `project_supervisor_permit()` on the supervisor's own workload gate; it is not in the operator gate, so `workload.place` of a project is still denied. One derivation names the revoked marker, `runtime_paths::revoked_marker(run_root, id)` (`<run_root>/<id>/revoked`): the `project.revoke` writer and the supervisor use the user daemon's run root (`project_cert_rpc::user_run_root`, which `post_boot` and `governance.parent.push` also use, so `$WEFTOS_RUNTIME_DIR` is honoured; with no run root the push is refused `no_run_root`), and the child reaches the same file as `RuntimePaths::revoked_marker()` of its run dir. Before this, the writer derived it from the manifest store (`<manifests>/../run/<id>/revoked`), which a `$WEFTOS_RUNTIME_DIR` or a moved store pointed elsewhere. The end-to-end test with a real child found two more wiring gaps, both fixed: a child's chain was signed by a fresh `chain.key` beside the checkpoint instead of `project.key`, and `post_boot`'s one-key check then stopped every real child (boot now signs a `project`-profile chain with the project key `pre_boot` handed over as the node key seed, confirmed against `project.key` by the strict reader; a missing seed, a missing file or a different key refuses the boot, and nothing is created), and a child was bound to no project (`compute_bound` now binds `RootSource::Child` to its id), so its handshake failed the supervisor's readiness check, a forward header (the graceful `kernel.shutdown`) could not verify, and governance events carried no `project_id`.
99:  - *Phase 2 review (`docs/research/daemon-topology/phase-2-review.md`), fixed before merge.* (M1) An overlay's `human_approval_required = true` over a parent that does not set it re-tags the parent's blocking rules as overlay denies (`OVERLAY_DENY_TAG`, which the engine never escalates, now also honoured on the browser-policy and binding-thread paths), so the flag makes only parent-permitted actions ask for approval and can never turn a parent deny into a `Defer` prompt approvable with `agent.chat.defer_decide`. The A7 overlay bullet's "booleans `parent OR overlay`" stays true; this is what makes it tighten-only. (M3) `governance`, `project` and `project.supervisor` are kernel-only sources (`chain::KERNEL_SOURCES`): `chain.append` and `chain_bridge` refuse them (`reserved_source`), and the rollback floor reads `governance.overlay.applied` only under source `governance`, so a `Write` caller cannot forge a huge `parent_version` and brick boot and reload. Replication (`append_signed`) still accepts them, because every chain carries governance events; a project kernel replicates nothing. (S1) Revoke is terminal for the id: `plan_registration` refuses any key for a project with a revoked (not merely rekey-retired) key and no certificate in force (`project_revoked`); a late store failure in `project.revoke` or `project.rekey` (cert file) is `identity_change_incomplete`, and the after-hooks (marker, session drop, child stop) run anyway. To run a revoked tree again, give it a new identity (`weft project init --fork --force`, after deleting the old `project.key` and certificate and moving the chain aside). `weft` keeps the user daemon's `error_kind` when an on-demand start is refused.
100:  - *Chain-authority follow-ups (after the Phase 2 and Phase 3 reviews).* (a) `auth.token` joins `chain::RESERVED_SOURCES`, the one list of authority-owned sources (`user.projects`, `project.anchor`, `auth.token`); a test pins each literal to its owning module's constant. `chain.append`, `chain_bridge` and `append_signed` refuse them, so a `Write` caller cannot forge a token issue or revoke and a peer's replicated `auth.token` event is refused, not folded (`append_signed` now checks the source before any sequence or fork bookkeeping). Per-event signatures do not exist on the chain, so readers cannot tell a replicated event from a local one; the refusal at the doors is the control, and authority-bearing chain replication is unsupported until a verified replication path exists: `MeshRuntime::handle_chain_sync_outcome` stops cleanly at the first authority event with a typed, non-fatal `StoppedAtAuthorityEvent {sequence, source}` (skipping would leave a sequence gap), and verified replication of authority events is future work. `chain::RESERVED_KINDS` (`governance.overlay.applied`, which the rollback floor and `user_pin_used` fold) is refused from replication under any source. The kernel sources (`governance`, `project`, `project.supervisor`) still replicate: the invariant is that a kernel that evaluates the overlay rollback floor never replicates. (b) Token revocation pruning uses the horizon `min(now, newest chain event time)`, so a boot with a bad clock (a Pi with a dead RTC) cannot drop a revocation of a token still live by the real clock, and the journal lock is held across the journal read and the rewrite. Pruning lags by one boot. (c) The overlay's `max_processes` and `spawn_budget` are consumed at project-kernel boot (`Prepared::apply_limits`), and the supervisor now exports the parent's real caps (its kernel config's `max_processes` and subagent `max_per_conv`) in `parent-policy.json` instead of `Limits::default()`, so a child's merged limits start from them and an overlay can only tighten; `governance.parent.push` seeds from the same caps and explicit `limits` only tighten on top. An overlay above the parent cap is refused with the parent value and the fix in the message. (d) The placement control plane's gate is fixed when it is built and does not follow a governance push (D8: applies after a restart); after a push the placement RPCs now refuse (`governance changed ... restart this kernel`) rather than let the older gate decide. (e) Signing rules for untagged signers: a signer that takes caller-chosen bytes and does not domain-tag them (the chain's `dual_sign`, the tree manager's `sign_bytes`, the mesh handshake) must never sign bytes that start with a `weftos-project-*-v1` tag; `chain::signs_project_domain` makes the first two refuse (release builds too). The mesh admission hello only signs its own domain-tagged layout, never caller bytes. JavaScript verifiers must read any `u64` above 2^53 (versions, sequence numbers, millisecond stamps) as a decimal string, since a JS number loses it.
101:  - *Phase 2 follow-ups, supervisor and shared services (cards 7e8d7350, d302d81c, 3503efb6).* (S5) `weaver kernel start --legacy-project-daemon` in a tree whose manifest says `via = child-kernel` is refused whatever the flag says, naming `weaver kernel start --project <id>` and the way back (`migrate-kernel --revert`); the flag still lifts only the "a user daemon is running" refusal. (S6) The supervisor records the build a child reports in its handshake (`sha`, `version`) in `<run>/<id>/state.json` when the child becomes ready or is adopted; `project.status` carries `kernel_sha`, `kernel_version` and `stale_build` (the running child's build differs from the daemon's), `weaver kernel status` annotates the row, and `weaver doctor` raises `child:<id>:stale-build`. Nothing restarts a stale child on its own: a project in use is never interrupted for an upgrade. (S7) `ensure_running` on a child in an automatic restart waits (bounded by `ready_timeout`) for its handshake instead of returning the unbound socket; adoption retries children that hold their lock but have not bound their socket for up to `ready_timeout`, and `stop_all` re-scans such leftovers first, so the cascade does not skip a child that was mid-boot. (S8) A degraded child that holds a spawn nonce the restarted daemon no longer expects (`spawn_not_expected`, `bad_spawn_nonce`, `spawn_expired`) drops the nonce and registers the way an adopted child does; a `running` child whose registry session has stayed expired for `lost_heartbeat_grace` (30 s, after the three missed beats) is treated as a crash: stopped with the pid re-verified, restarted inside its restart budget, then `failed`. The ledger pid (S8c) was fixed with the review round. (Note b) `prepare` consults the revocation view: a revoked project with no certificate in force is refused (`project_revoked`) even when its marker is missing, so it is not spawned five times to burn the restart budget. (d302d81c 2, 4) `project.stop` on an adopted-but-refused leftover answers `unmanaged_pid` (the CLI prints "unmanaged kernel pid N"); the idle clock counts a call only after the capability check and the project-token allow-list passed. (3503efb6) `shared.*` re-checks on every call that the project is still registered and active (limits stay cached until `shared.reload`, so editing a manifest still does not raise them), a re-registration invalidates that project's cached limits, and the four global slots are fair: a project that holds a slot may not take another while a project that holds none was refused within 3 s, so two busy projects cannot keep a third out. Declined: a `daemon::run` smoke test for the project profile with decoys beyond what `shared_services_child.rs` and the end-to-end test's real child already prove (the heavy half needs the real `weaver` binary and is the e2e's job); the `.env`-in-project-root case (S10e) stays open. Review fixes: an adopted child's tombstone session is never a lost heartbeat (it is flagged `adopted` until the child registers), a child whose last beat was busy is not restarted, and one restart happens per liveness pass; the boot scan waits at most 3 s and `stop_all` 2 s for children that have not bound their socket; the S5 guard ignores archived manifests; the `shared.*` active check is a `stat` on a blocking thread (the manifest is read only when its mtime or length changed) and an unreadable store is `project_store_unavailable`; the fairness wait window is 10 s. Known limits: a child that was unregistered from the store keeps running until stopped (the supervisor does not watch the store), and the `unmanaged_pid` report of `project.stop` is not re-verified at answer time (the pid may have exited since the last scan).
106:  - *D14 handshake shape:* beyond `{node_id, user_id, project_id, depth, parent}` it carries `proto` (a range), `user_key_id` (hash of the chain verifying key), `profile`, `roles`, `bound_via`, `runtime_dir`, `pid`, `version`, `sha` and `binary`. `user_id` is the local uid, unverified until D3 (Phase 3).
124:  - *Stale keys:* after `bind rebind` or `revoke` the old daemon keeps retrying at its slowest backoff (30 s) instead of burning its uid's registration budget; it does not recover on its own and needs a restart with the current user key. A transport failure returns it to the normal backoff.
161:  *Recommendation (a proposal, not a decision):* rename the Phase 2 child link to **project-link** (tag `project-link/1`, methods `project.link.*` or keep `mesh.*` as aliases for one release, new PoP domain tag with a version bump), because it is the project kernel's link to its parent and does not touch the mesh. Keep `mesh-local` for the service link, where the name describes the machine-local mesh path. Follow-up card needed; nothing was renamed, and a renamed PoP domain tag changes what a child signs when it registers, so the rename needs a compatibility window between a user daemon and children of an older build.
170:  - *Child peers (P2 S9).* ADR-070's same-uid shortcut honoured a literal `auth: "admin"` from any peer with the daemon's uid, which includes a compromised supervised child, which could then mint an owner token. The user daemon now classifies each unix-socket peer at accept (`child_peer`): a same-uid peer whose process group is a supervised child's (children lead their own group, `process_group(0)`, so the group id is the child's pid; the supervisor exposes `Launcher::supervised_pids`) is `Child`. A same-uid peer whose pid is unknown or whose process group cannot be read (it exited and was reaped before accept) is also a `Child`: the classifier fails closed. The supervised set is every child process group this daemon started or adopted, kept until `killpg(pgid, 0)` reports `ESRCH` (so grandchildren orphaned by the leader's exit are still recognised; the check never reaps). The Windows named-pipe path and the legacy `handle_connection` wrapper do not classify and default to Owner. A `Child`'s literal scopes are ignored and it is treated as anonymous (read and chat), not refused, because a child's own parent calls (`kernel.handshake`, `mesh.*`, `project.anchor.submit`) carry the client library's implicit `"admin"` and need only `read`; only its project token is honoured, and that token is write-only and method-restricted (`project_token_method_denied`). The owner's CLI is in no child's group and is unchanged. *Not done, and why:* stripping `wft_` tokens in the TCP relay. No code path carries a token into a child (the user daemon calls a child only with a signed forward header), remote callers legitimately authenticate with `wft_` tokens (ADR-102 D3), and with the mint hop closed a child cannot obtain an owner token to relay. *Honest limit:* this is a narrowing, not a boundary. A child process that leaves its group (`setsid`, `setpgid`, a double fork) escapes it; the boundary is a separate uid or a Phase 4 sandbox.
